When creating Databricks connection with AWS it creates a S3 Bucket and IAM Roles:

Why a new S3 bucket is created:

Databricks needs a workspace root storage bucket (sometimes called “workspace assets storage” or “root S3 bucket”) for Databricks-managed stuff like:
  - notebook/workspace metadata and artifacts
  - cluster logs
  - library installs / init scripts (depending on setup)
  - system files needed for the workspace to function
  - sometimes managed Delta table storage (depending on features)

This bucket is not your data lake. It’s “Databricks’ home folder” in your AWS account.

Why a new S3 bucket is created:

Databricks separates permissions on purpose:
1) Compute (cross-account) role
This lets Databricks launch and manage EC2 clusters in your AWS account:
  - create/attach network interfaces
  - security groups
  - spin up worker nodes
  - read instance profiles / etc.
Without this, you literally can’t run PySpark in Databricks.

2) Storage access role
This lets Databricks access S3 (workspace bucket + any data buckets you allow).
Databricks keeps “launch compute” permissions separate from “access data” permissions for security and least privilege.

